Defination - An Object is a Instance of a class used to model realworld entities.

Q) What it possess?
    An object contains
    1) State - Represents Properties 
    2)Behaviour - Represents the functionality of what an object does.
    3)Identity - a unique identifier used to make each object unique from one another.

    class Student { 
        int age;// State
         String name; // State 
         void study() { // Behavior 
            System.out.println("Student is studying");
        }
     } 
     class Main {
         public static void main(String[] args) {
             Student s1 = new Student();
              Student s2 = new Student(); 
              s1.age = 13; 
              s1.name = "xx"; 
              s2.age = 12; 
              s2.name = "xz"; 
            } 
        }

Q) How do we create an object?
   Using new keyword during runtime execution of the prgm.

Q) What exactly happens when we use new?
        Internal Process of Object Creation & a Constructor call
---------------------------------------------------------
      Student s1 = new Student();

    new Student() // new keyword used to create object in the heap memory 
      (step 1 - JVM allocates memory for the object in the heap,
       step 2 - Objects instance variables are assigned with default values.)

        ↓

    Student() constructor executes
    (step 3 - constructor gets invoked to assign object values,
     step 4 - Object gets initialized with its valess.)
      
      ↓

     s1 // reference variable
     (step 5 - s1 is a reference variable that holds a reference to the object.
Q) What is the difference between an object and a reference?
  Object -> contains actual data, stored in heap memory, runtime exec
  Reference -> refers to an object , stores in stack memory. 